INT. TAXIDERMY SHOP - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Glass eyes in a coffee tin. A badger mid-snarl. A half-stuffed owl on the workbench, wings pinned.

MAG SOLBERG, 60s, rubber gloves, stitches the owl without looking at her visitors. A landline phone sits next to her, cord stretched.

ISAIAH TWOBEARS, 30s, leather jacket, stands too near the door. His hands are in his pockets. He hasn't taken off his gloves either.

DET. NADIA KHALIL, 40s, drifts along a shelf of foxes. Touches nothing.

KHALIL
Nice work.

MAG
Thanks.

KHALIL
You do the owl for the bar on Delancey?

MAG
Does it look like I do bars?

Khalil lifts a fox. Puts it back facing the wall.

ISAIAH
We should go.

KHALIL
We just got here.

The phone RINGS. Mag lets it. Three rings. Four. Isaiah shifts his weight.

KHALIL (CONT'D)
Get that.

Mag peels off one glove. Answers.

MAG
Solberg's.

GUS (V.O.)
(through the receiver)
Mags. They find the thing yet?

Mag looks at Khalil. Khalil smiles. Mag looks at the owl.

MAG
Wrong number.

GUS (V.O.)
Mags--

She hangs up. Picks up the needle again. The thread trembles, once.

KHALIL
Who was that?

MAG
Somebody's husband.

Isaiah steps out through the back door. Cold air rushes in. Khalil watches him go and does nothing.

KHALIL
Finish the owl, Mag.

Mag pulls the thread tight.
